<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of al-Nuri (Mosul)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1172">1172</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-al-nuri">Great Mosque of al-Nuri</a> was a mosque in the old city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, built by the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in 1172. Its leaning brick minaret, al-Hadba, &quot;the hunchback&quot;, became the symbol of the city. In 2014 the Islamic State leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi proclaimed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> there, and the group blew up the mosque in 2017; its reconstruction was agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-al-nuri-mosul.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of al-Nuri (Mosul)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: الدبوني · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_al-Nuri_Feb_2025_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that Nur ad-Din Zangi, a Turkoman atabeg of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>, built the mosque in 1172-1173, shortly before his death. According to the chronicle of Ibn al-Athir, after taking control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> he ordered his nephew Fakhr al-Din to build it. Nur ad-Din rode to the site, ordered that neighbouring houses and shops be added to the land but that nothing be taken without the willing agreement of the owners, and put Sheikh Umar al-Malla in charge. The properties were bought at substantial prices, and the building was completed in the year 568 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>, or 1172-1173. The mosque underwent many renovations over the years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-al-nuri">Great Mosque of al-Nuri</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in the Nineveh Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, known for its leaning minaret, al-Hadba, 'the hunchback', which gave the city its nickname. The cylindrical minaret stood 45 metres high, with seven bands of decorative brickwork in geometric patterns. By the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in the 14th century it was already listing and had its nickname. Its design follows a form developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and resembles minarets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar">Sinjar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbil</a>. According to local tradition the minaret bowed in reverence when Muhammad passed overhead on his ascent to heaven, while local Christian tradition says it bows towards the tomb of the Virgin Mary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosque, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and its tomb were dismantled and reassembled in 1942 in a restoration programme of the Iraqi government. Attempts to stabilise the minaret were made in 1981, and bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in the Iran-Iraq War worsened the lean. After the Islamic State occupied Mosul on 10 June 2014, Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> in the mosque on 4 July 2014 to declare a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. On 21 June 2017, during the Battle of Mosul, Iraqi government forces reported that the mosque had been blown up; the Islamic State's Amaq agency claimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> airstrike, a claim not substantiated, and an Iraqi video showed charges exploding inside the structure.</p><p>On 23 April 2018 the United Arab Emirates pledged US$50.4 million for reconstruction with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s culture ministry and ICCROM, and a foundation stone was laid on 17 December 2018. In April 2021 a group of eight Egyptian architects won a competition among 123 entries; the design was criticised as resembling Gulf architecture more than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. On 13 November 2024 workers fitted an Iraqi flag on top of the new minaret, marking the end of reconstruction, and an official opening was held on 1 September 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Mosque of al-Nuri, Mosul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Mosque of al-Nuri</b>, built by Nur al-Din in 1172 with its leaning al-Hadba minaret, stood until its destruction in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/state/zengid-dynasty-of-mosul">Zengid dynasty of Mosul</a></li></ul></section>
